| [scripts](scripts), [.github/workflows](.github/workflows) | Independent smoke/recovery, pin/security checks and CI |
| [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md), [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md) | Human and AI development workflow |

## Docker first

From this application root, with Docker BuildKit and Compose v2:

```sh
docker build -t @NAME@:local .
docker compose up -d --no-build --wait
curl --fail http://127.0.0.1:8080/health/ready
curl --fail http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/hello
```

Open http://localhost:8080. For another host port use
`HOST_PORT=8081 docker compose up -d --no-build --wait` in a POSIX shell. Container
port remains 8080. Compose uses project `@NAME@`; preserve its identity and actual
`light-data` volume across updates. Data lives at `/data/@NAME@.db`, including WAL/SHM.
UID/GID is 10001, the root filesystem is read-only, and only local writable persistent
storage is supported. One SQLite instance; serialize replacements. Do not use
`down --volumes` on deployment storage. HTTPS requires your trusted reverse proxy.

## Native development

Install [Git](https://git-scm.com/downloads/), [rustup](https://rustup.rs/) with the
pinned [Rust toolchain](backend/rust-toolchain.toml), a native C compiler/linker,
[Node.js](https://nodejs.org/en/download) matching [this pin](frontend/.node-version),
npm selected by [packageManager](frontend/package.json), and
[Python 3.11+](https://www.python.org/downloads/) with SQLite support.
Check `node --version`/`npm --version` against those files; from backend,
`rustup show active-toolchain` identifies the selected Rust environment.
Docker-only builds do not need host Rust/Node. From this application's root:

```sh
python3 scripts/check-toolchain-pins.py
cd frontend
npm ci --no-audit --no-fund
npm run build
cd ../backend
cargo run --locked
```

Keep that terminal running. In a second terminal, from this application's root:

```sh
cd frontend
npm run dev
```

Open http://localhost:5173 for Vite hot reload. Rust serves the last built frontend
at http://localhost:8080; build frontend once before starting Rust even when using
Vite, because startup checks for compiled assets. Rust code changes need a backend
restart; no backend watcher is installed. Vite proxies `/api` and `/health` to Rust.

Native defaults, when launched from `backend`, are `data/@NAME@.db` and
`../frontend/dist`. Rust reads process environment; `.env.example` is a reference,
not automatically loaded. `PORT` controls Rust; `API_PROXY_TARGET` controls Vite's
backend target. Docker `HOST_PORT` only controls the published port. See
[workflow differences and checks](CONTRIBUTING.md#2-run-and-understand-the-application)
and [troubleshooting](CONTRIBUTING.md#7-troubleshooting).

## Consistent backup and restore

Never copy only an active SQLite file. Follow the
[backup/recovery runbook](MAINTENANCE.md#3-create-and-retain-a-consistent-sqlite-backup),
restore into an independent volume, and preserve the original and off-host copies.
A successful backup, healthy container or passing scanner alone is not proof of
recoverability. [Updates and rollback](MAINTENANCE.md#releases-updates-and-recovery)
require explicit image retention and database compatibility assessment.

## Identity, security and license

Project/package/binary/image: `@NAME@`; Rust crate imports normalize hyphens to
underscores. Display name: @DISPLAY@. Keep the upstream [MIT notice](LICENSE);
add your own notices for your modifications. Configure your own private reporting
contact in [SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md). [SECURITY-SCANNING.md](SECURITY-SCANNING.md)
explains independent scanners, policy and reports. Tests/CI are included tools,
not evidence that your own checks or deployments have already passed.
